# Home Dashboard

A self-hosted dashboard for bills, medication reminders, calendar, news, and stocks,
with a voice assistant that also speaks reminders out loud. Built to run on a
Raspberry Pi and display full-screen on a monitor/TV.

## What you get
- **Bills & Medication**: pulled straight from your Google Calendar. Any event with
  words like "pill/med/dose/rx" in the title shows under Medication; anything with
  "bill/pay/due/invoice/rent/mortgage/utility" shows under Bills. Everything else
  shows under Upcoming.
- **News**: live headlines (Google News RSS, no API key needed).
- **Stocks**: live quotes via Stooq (free, no API key needed).
- **Voice assistant**: tap the mic and ask "what are my bills", "any meds today",
  "what's the news", "how's the market", "what's on my calendar". It also
  proactively **speaks** medication/bill reminders 30 minutes before they're due.

---

## 1. Google Calendar setup (one-time)

1. Go to https://console.cloud.google.com/ and create a project (or use an existing one).
2. Enable the **Google Calendar API** (APIs & Services → Library → search "Google Calendar API" → Enable).
3. Go to APIs & Services → Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID.
   - Application type: **Web application**
   - Authorized redirect URI: `http://localhost:3000/oauth2callback` (for local testing)
     — for the live Heroku deployment, use `https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com/oauth2callback` instead (see section 5).
4. Copy the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.
5. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`.

## 2. Install & run locally (optional, for testing before deploying)

On your Raspberry Pi (Raspberry Pi OS, 64-bit recommended):

```bash
# Install Node.js 20 (if not already installed)
curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt-get install -y nodejs

cd home-dashboard
npm install
cp .env.example .env   # then edit .env with your Google credentials
node server.js
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/auth` in a browser once, log into Google, and grant
calendar access. The server terminal will print a `GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN` — paste
that into your `.env` file, then restart the server (`node server.js`). You only
need to do this once; the token keeps refreshing itself after that.

Now visit `http://localhost:3000` — you should see your dashboard.

## 3. Add bills & meds to Google Calendar

Just create normal calendar events, e.g.:
- "Pay electric bill" (recurring monthly) → shows as a Bill
- "Take blood pressure pill" (recurring daily, 8am) → shows as a Medication reminder

The dashboard re-checks every minute and will speak a reminder ~30 min before
each one, once.

## 4. Display it full-screen on your Pi monitor (kiosk mode)

Install Chromium if you don't have it:
```bash
sudo apt-get install -y chromium-browser
```

Make sure a microphone is plugged into the Pi if you want voice input (a cheap
USB mic works fine). Web Speech API requires Chrome/Chromium.

**Auto-start dashboard server + browser in kiosk mode on boot:**

Create a systemd service for the server:
```bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/home-dashboard.service << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Home Dashboard Server
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/usr/bin/node /home/pi/home-dashboard/server.js
WorkingDirectory=/home/pi/home-dashboard
Restart=always
User=pi
EnvironmentFile=/home/pi/home-dashboard/.env

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

sudo systemctl enable home-dashboard
sudo systemctl start home-dashboard
```

Then autostart Chromium in kiosk mode on the desktop (Raspberry Pi OS with
desktop). Edit `~/.config/lxsession/LXDE-pi/autostart` (create it if missing)
and add:
```
@xset s off
@xset -dpms
@xset s noblank
@chromium-browser --kiosk --noerrdialogs --disable-infobars --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required --use-fake-ui-for-media-stream https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com
```
(Use `http://localhost:3000` here only if you're running the server locally on
the Pi instead of via Heroku — see section 5 for the Heroku deploy.)
- `--use-fake-ui-for-media-stream` auto-grants mic permission so it doesn't
  pop up a prompt on a screen with no keyboard attached.
- Reboot the Pi — it should boot straight into the dashboard, full-screen.

Note: you don't need to run the `home-dashboard.service` systemd unit on the
Pi at all if you're using the Heroku deployment — that service is only for
running the server locally instead of on Heroku.

## 5. Deploying to Heroku

This app is deployed at **https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com** (app name: `homedashboard`).

```bash
# from the home-dashboard folder


Set your config vars (these replace the `.env` file):
```bash
heroku config:set GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com -a homedashboard
heroku config:set GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret -a homedashboard
heroku config:set GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com/oauth2callback -a homedashboard
```

In Google Cloud Console (APIs & Services → Credentials → your OAuth client):
- **Authorized JavaScript origins**: `https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com` (no path, no trailing slash)
- **Authorized redirect URIs**: `https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com/oauth2callback`

Visit `https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com/auth` once to authorize, copy the
refresh token Heroku prints in the logs:
```bash
heroku logs --tail -a homedashboard
```
then:
```bash
heroku config:set GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=the-token-you-copied -a homedashboard
```

On your Pi, point Chromium's kiosk mode at the Heroku URL:
```
@chromium-browser --kiosk --noerrdialogs --disable-infobars --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required --use-fake-ui-for-media-stream https://homedashboard.herokuapp.com
```

Note: Heroku's free tier was discontinued — the Eco or Basic dyno plan
(a few dollars/month) is the cheapest option that stays awake reliably for a
dashboard you want always-on.

## 6. Monitor choice

Any HDMI monitor works. For a wall-mounted "smart display" feel, a lot of
people use a 10–15" portrait or landscape monitor; the layout here is
landscape-first. If you want portrait, we can adjust the CSS grid.

## Notes & next steps
- Stock symbols are hardcoded to AAPL, MSFT, GOOGL, SPY in `app.js` — change
  the `symbols=` list in `loadStocks()` to whatever you want to track.
- News defaults to general US headlines; you can adjust the RSS query in
  `server.js` (`/api/news`) to filter by topic.
- Everything runs locally on your Pi — no cloud service required except your
  own Google account and the free public RSS/stock feeds.